    "text": "this is the original prompt:\n\nTITLE: [your word]  \\\nPROJECT: [brand / exhibition / campaign]  \\\nMATERIAL: [facade skin / elastic membrane / stone / plaster / earth / other]  \\\nTRANSFORMATION: [peeling / swelling / erosion / cracking / other]  \\\nHIDDEN LAYER: [color / material / none]  \\\nENVIRONMENT: [museum / outdoor plaza / desert / architecture / other]  \\\nSUBJECT: [small human figure / none]  \\\nMAIN COLORS: [2–3 colors]  \\\nFORMAT: 9:16 vertical poster\\\n\\\nCreate a contemporary material typography poster where the main word is physically generated by the transformation of the material itself.\\\n\\\nDo not simply print or place the word onto the surface.\\\n\\\nThe typography should emerge through a real material process such as peeling, pneumatic expansion, erosion, cracking, splitting, melting, compression or another believable transformation.\\\n\\\nThe main title should occupy roughly 40–60% of the composition and remain bold, simple and highly readable at thumbnail size.\\\n\\\nUse large-scale material changes rather than many small decorative details.\\\n\\\nFor peeling, let the outer surface physically lift, curl and separate to reveal a contrasting layer underneath, with the exposed areas forming the typography.\\\n\\\nFor swelling, let internal pressure push a continuous membrane outward so its inflated volumes and pressure valleys create the word.\\\n\\\nFor erosion, remove the outer material through wind, water or weathering so a contrasting inner layer is gradually revealed as readable type.\\\n\\\nFor cracking, let large fractures, broken edges and displaced surface pieces expose a contrasting internal material that forms the word.\\\n\\\nThe transformation should remain physically believable. Show realistic material thickness, tension, depth, shadows, exposed layers, deformation and surface behavior.\\\n\\\nKeep the word integrated into one continuous material system. Avoid making each letter a separate 3D object.\\\n\\\nUse one dominant material color, one strong contrasting revealed color, and a restrained architectural or natural environment.\\\n\\\nThe first visual impression should come from large color fields and a clear oversized word. The second impression should reveal how the material transformation actually creates the typography.\\\n\\\nIf a person is included, keep them small and use them mainly as a scale reference. Their pose should feel natural and secondary to the installation.\\\n\\\nAdd only minimal editorial information such as project name, study title, location, date and one short conceptual line.\\\n\\\nOverall style: material transformation, experimental typography, contemporary installation art, spatial graphic design, architectural photography, editorial poster, strong physicality, clear hierarchy and stop-scroll visual impact.\\\n\\\nAvoid flat pasted text, ordinary 3D letters, random damage, excessive debris, weak material logic, tiny decorative details, neon sci-fi styling, cluttered environments, excessive filler copy, watermarks and numbering.\\",
    "readable_text": "this is the original prompt:\n\n\n\nTITLE: [your word]  \nPROJECT: [brand / exhibition / campaign]  \nMATERIAL: [facade skin / elastic membrane / stone / plaster / earth / other]  \nTRANSFORMATION: [peeling / swelling / erosion / cracking / other]  \nHIDDEN LAYER: [color / material / none]  \nENVIRONMENT: [museum / outdoor plaza / desert / architecture / other]  \nSUBJECT: [small human figure / none]  \nMAIN COLORS: [2–3 colors]  \nFORMAT: 9:16 vertical poster\n\nCreate a contemporary material typography poster where the main word is physically generated by the transformation of the material itself.\n\nDo not simply print or place the word onto the surface.\n\nThe typography should emerge through a real material process such as peeling, pneumatic expansion, erosion, cracking, splitting, melting, compression or another believable transformation.\n\nThe main title should occupy roughly 40–60% of the composition and remain bold, simple and highly readable at thumbnail size.\n\nUse large-scale material changes rather than many small decorative details.\n\nFor peeling, let the outer surface physically lift, curl and separate to reveal a contrasting layer underneath, with the exposed areas forming the typography.\n\nFor swelling, let internal pressure push a continuous membrane outward so its inflated volumes and pressure valleys create the word.\n\nFor erosion, remove the outer material through wind, water or weathering so a contrasting inner layer is gradually revealed as readable type.\n\nFor cracking, let large fractures, broken edges and displaced surface pieces expose a contrasting internal material that forms the word.\n\nThe transformation should remain physically believable. Show realistic material thickness, tension, depth, shadows, exposed layers, deformation and surface behavior.\n\nKeep the word integrated into one continuous material system. Avoid making each letter a separate 3D object.\n\nUse one dominant material color, one strong contrasting revealed color, and a restrained architectural or natural environment.\n\nThe first visual impression should come from large color fields and a clear oversized word. The second impression should reveal how the material transformation actually creates the typography.\n\nIf a person is included, keep them small and use them mainly as a scale reference. Their pose should feel natural and secondary to the installation.\n\nAdd only minimal editorial information such as project name, study title, location, date and one short conceptual line.\n\nOverall style: material transformation, experimental typography, contemporary installation art, spatial graphic design, architectural photography, editorial poster, strong physicality, clear hierarchy and stop-scroll visual impact.\n\nAvoid flat pasted text, ordinary 3D letters, random damage, excessive debris, weak material logic, tiny decorative details, neon sci-fi styling, cluttered environments, excessive filler copy, watermarks and numbering.\n",
