    "text": "now this:\n\nUse case: ads-marketing\nAsset type: Typography-led layered paper-cut editorial poster\n\nINPUTS\n\nTheme:\n[FASHION / OCEAN / MEMORY / ARCHITECTURE / SCIENCE / CULTURE / OTHER]\n\nCampaign purpose:\n[EDITORIAL / EVENT / AWARENESS / THOUGHT LEADERSHIP / EXHIBITION / BRAND CAMPAIGN]\n\nMain title:\n[YOUR TITLE]\n\nKey statement:\n[SHORT CENTRAL MESSAGE]\n\nMain silhouette:\n[DRESS / WHALE / PLANET / HUMAN PROFILE / ARCH / BUILDING / OBJECT / OTHER RECOGNIZABLE SHAPE]\n\nInner world:\n[RUNWAY / DEEP OCEAN / MEMORY SPACE / COURTYARD / LABORATORY / CITY / LANDSCAPE / OTHER SCENE]\n\nColor palette:\n[THREE TO FIVE RESTRAINED COLORS]\n\nEditorial tone:\n[FASHION / CULTURAL / ARTISTIC / SCIENTIFIC / ARCHITECTURAL]\n\nType interaction:\n[BEHIND / OVERLAPPING / WRAPPING / CROPPED / PASSING THROUGH THE SILHOUETTE]\n\nInformation graphic:\n[NONE / TIMELINE / PROGRESSION / COMPARISON / CIRCULAR RELATIONSHIP / CONCEPTUAL PROCESS / SIMPLE GRAPH]\n\nInformation content:\n[EXACT LABELS, VALUES, OR CONCEPTUAL RELATIONSHIPS]\n\nAspect ratio:\n[9:16 / 4:5 / 1:1 / OTHER]\n\n\nPRIMARY REQUEST\n\nCreate a refined, typography-led editorial poster combining one recognizable layered paper-cut silhouette with bold campaign messaging and, when requested, one restrained information graphic.\n\nThe result should feel like an art magazine cover, cultural campaign, exhibition poster, fashion editorial, or sophisticated thought-leadership graphic—not a craft project or generic social-media template.\n\n\nVISUAL PRIORITY\n\nBalance the composition approximately as follows:\n\n- Typography and written information: 45–55%\n- Main paper-cut silhouette: 35–45%\n- Hidden inner world and supporting graphic: 10–20%\n\nMaintain this visual hierarchy:\n\n1. Main title\n2. Bold silhouette\n3. Key statement\n4. Layered depth and hidden world\n5. Supporting information or diagram\n\n\nMAIN SILHOUETTE\n\nBuild the composition around one bold, immediately recognizable silhouette.\n\nThe silhouette should remain readable from a distance and may:\n\n- Crop beyond the poster edges\n- Interact with the headline\n- Divide the page into text and image fields\n- Contain an opening for the inner world\n- Partially hide behind typography\n- Wrap around a chart or diagram\n\nAvoid presenting the silhouette as an isolated object centered inside a decorative frame.\n\n\nPAPER-CUT STRUCTURE\n\nInside the silhouette, create six to nine layers of cut paper that gradually open into a deeper space.\n\nEach layer should follow the general form of the silhouette while changing naturally in:\n\n- Scale\n- Direction\n- Contour\n- Opening width\n- Visual rhythm\n- Relationship to the typography\n\nAvoid perfectly even, symmetrical, or mechanical concentric layers.\n\nUse:\n\n- Matte art-paper surfaces\n- Clean cut edges\n- Subtle natural paper fibers\n- Soft, shallow shadows\n- Visually flat editorial depth\n\nThe result should feel physically crafted from paper, but professionally art-directed.\n\n\nHIDDEN INNER WORLD\n\nAt the deepest point, reveal a small narrative environment connected to the theme.\n\nThe inner world may include:\n\n- A runway\n- An underwater environment\n- A memory landscape\n- A courtyard\n- A city\n- A laboratory\n- An observation platform\n- Another meaningful setting\n\nWhen appropriate, include one very small human figure to establish scale and discovery.\n\nThe hidden world must remain secondary to the silhouette and typography.\n\n\nTYPOGRAPHY\n\nTypography must be integrated into the paper structure rather than placed separately above or below it.\n\nUse one expressive editorial display typeface for the main title and one restrained supporting typeface for secondary information.\n\nAllow the title to:\n\n- Pass behind outer paper layers\n- Overlap the silhouette\n- Wrap around the silhouette’s contour\n- Extend beyond the poster edges\n- Become selectively cropped\n- Appear through openings between layers\n- Align with the direction of the paper contours\n\nThe title must remain readable at thumbnail size.\n\nUse supporting text sparingly and maintain generous negative space.\n\n\nINFORMATION GRAPHICS\n\nWhen requested, include no more than one primary diagram and one very small secondary indicator.\n\nSuitable options include:\n\n- A simple timeline following a paper layer\n- A circular relationship diagram inside the silhouette\n- A three-stage conceptual progression\n- A restrained line graph\n- A small bar comparison\n- Connected nodes moving into the inner world\n- A journey or discovery path\n- A before-and-after relationship\n- A hierarchy represented by paper depth\n\nIntegrate the diagram into the paper layers. Do not place it inside a separate dashboard or rectangular infographic panel.\n\nUse thin lines, clear labels, simple shapes, and colors from the supplied palette.\n\n\nDATA ACCURACY\n\nIf numerical values are provided, reproduce them accurately.\n\nIf no numerical data is provided, create a clearly conceptual diagram without invented percentages, measurements, statistics, dates, axis values, or scientific claims.\n\nDo not imply that decorative shapes represent real data unless values are supplied.\n\n\nFINAL TEXT — VERBATIM\n\nUse only the following text. Preserve spelling, capitalization, punctuation, and wording exactly.\n\nMain title:\n\"[MAIN TITLE]\"\n\nKey statement:\n\"[KEY STATEMENT]\"\n\nSupporting copy:\n\"[SUPPORTING COPY]\"\n\nDiagram title:\n\"[DIAGRAM TITLE]\"\n\nDiagram labels:\n\"[DIAGRAM LABELS]\"\n\nProject or issue name:\n\"[PROJECT OR ISSUE NAME]\"\n\nDate:\n\"[DATE]\"\n\nLocation:\n\"[LOCATION]\"\n\nCall to action:\n\"[CALL TO ACTION]\"\n\n\nCOLOR\n\nUse a restrained palette of three to five colors.\n\nMove from lighter outer layers toward deeper, darker inner layers while maintaining clear separation between each paper layer.\n\nUse clean, solid colors. Avoid digital gradients, distressed overlays, metallic effects, and excessive texture.\n\nReserve the strongest color for one or two of the following:\n\n- A key word in the title\n- The deepest paper layer\n- The narrative focal point\n- A diagram line or data point\n- The call to action\n\n\nCOMPOSITION\n\nLeave generous negative space for typography and supporting information.\n\nThe composition may be:\n\n- Asymmetric\n- Vertically stacked\n- Diagonally structured\n- Built around a central void\n- Split between typography and silhouette\n- Organized around a silhouette that crosses the page\n\nThe silhouette does not always need to occupy the center.\n\nBuild the layout around the shape of the silhouette, the length of the headline, and the direction of the information graphic.\n\n\nCONSTRAINTS\n\n- Preserve the requested aspect ratio without stretching or distortion.\n- Keep the main silhouette instantly recognizable.\n- Use six to nine paper layers.\n- Use only the supplied text and data.\n- Keep the headline readable at thumbnail size.\n- Use no more than two information graphics.\n- Maintain clear negative space and hierarchy.\n- Keep shadows shallow and physically believable.\n- Produce a complete campaign poster, not a reusable-looking template.\n\n\nAVOID\n\nHeavy 3D rendering, foam board, plastic surfaces, CGI depth, glossy materials, perfectly even concentric layers, mechanical symmetry, excessive shadows, digital gradients, photorealistic environments dominating the poster, multiple competing silhouettes, dense dashboards, complicated charts, invented statistics, generic filler copy, meaningless microtext, decorative numbering, “01/02,” “Vol.,” four-point sparkles, excessive icons, logos, watermarks, borders, collage panels, and decorative clutter.",
